I found the important shape seam: the source corpus stores one row per `(user, candidate action)`, not one state row per user. To preserve a true user-axis transform without weighting users by how many candidates they have, I’m defining each live population as users sharing the same anchor and candidate action identity (with null as its own identity). That is the action-conditioned analogue of the sibling’s per-step user-axis transform; every user contributes at most once to each population, and the heads still perform the final masked argmax independently. Constant populations will emit exact `+0.0` in the z block.
